-- Seed rich educational content for all chapters in synapse_ai
USE synapse_ai;

INSERT INTO chapter_lessons (chapter_id, subject_id, part_number, subtitle_tag, headline, concept_text, key_points, example_title, example_problem, example_solution, quick_example_text, ai_recommendation)
VALUES
-- Chapter 1: Mathematics - Quadratic Equations
(1, 1, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'What is a Quadratic Equation?', 'A quadratic equation is a second-order polynomial equation in a single variable x, with a non-zero coefficient for x².', 'Standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0.\nDegree is always 2.\nCan have 2 real roots, 1 repeated root, or 2 complex roots.', 'Quick Example', 'Solve: x² - 5x + 6 = 0', 'Factoring gives (x - 2)(x - 3) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 3.', 'x² - 5x + 6 = 0 factors into (x - 2)(x - 3) = 0.', 'Practice factoring trinomials where a = 1 first.'),
(1, 1, 2, 'Part 2: The Quadratic Formula', 'Solving by Formula and Discriminant', 'When factoring is difficult, the universal quadratic formula provides exact roots using the discriminant Δ = b² - 4ac.', 'Formula: x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / (2a).\nIf Δ > 0: Two distinct real roots.\nIf Δ = 0: One repeated real root.\nIf Δ < 0: Two complex roots.', 'Quick Example', 'Compute discriminant for 2x² + 4x + 2 = 0', 'Δ = 4² - 4(2)(2) = 16 - 16 = 0 (One repeated root: x = -1).', 'Discriminant Δ = b² - 4ac reveals the nature of roots instantly.', 'Use completing the square to understand how the formula is derived.'),

-- Chapter 2: Mathematics - Differential Calculus
(2, 1, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'Limits & Derivatives', 'Calculus studies continuous change. The derivative represents the instantaneous rate of change of a function with respect to one of its variables.', 'Derivative definition: f\'(x) = lim(h->0) [f(x+h) - f(x)] / h.\nPower rule: d/dx(x^n) = n * x^(n-1).\nRepresents slope of tangent line to the curve.', 'Quick Example', 'Find derivative of f(x) = 3x² + 5x', 'f\'(x) = 6x + 5 using power rule and sum rule.', 'Slope of y = x² at x = 3 is 2(3) = 6.', 'Visualize derivatives as speedometer readings in physics.'),
(2, 1, 2, 'Part 2: Advanced Techniques', 'Product, Quotient & Chain Rules', 'Complex mathematical functions are differentiated using structured composition rules.', 'Product rule: (u*v)\' = u\'v + uv\'.\nQuotient rule: (u/v)\' = (u\'v - uv\') / v².\nChain rule: d/dx[f(g(x))] = f\'(g(x)) * g\'(x).', 'Quick Example', 'Differentiate y = (2x + 1)³', 'dy/dx = 3(2x + 1)² * 2 = 6(2x + 1)²', 'Outer derivative multiplied by inner derivative.', 'Master the chain rule as it is essential for neural network backpropagation.'),

-- Chapter 3: Mathematics - Linear Algebra & Matrices
(3, 1, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'Vectors & Matrix Operations', 'Matrices are rectangular arrays of numbers that represent linear transformations between vector spaces.', 'Matrix multiplication requires columns of A to match rows of B.\nTranspose flips rows and columns: (A^T)_ij = A_ji.\nIdentity matrix I acts as the number 1.', 'Quick Example', 'Multiply [[1, 2], [3, 4]] by [[1], [0]]', 'Result is [[1], [3]].', 'Vector transformations scale and rotate geometric spaces.', 'Linear algebra is the foundational language of machine learning.'),
(3, 1, 2, 'Part 2: Core Concepts', 'Determinants & Matrix Inverses', 'The determinant measures the scaling factor of a linear transformation, and determines whether an inverse exists.', 'det(A) ≠ 0 means matrix is invertible (non-singular).\nFor 2x2: det([[a,b],[c,d]]) = ad - bc.\nInverse: A^(-1) = (1/det(A)) * adj(A).', 'Quick Example', 'Find det([[2, 1], [4, 3]])', 'det = 2*3 - 1*4 = 6 - 4 = 2.', 'A non-zero determinant guarantees unique solutions to linear systems.', 'Practice Gaussian elimination for higher order systems.'),

-- Chapter 4: Mathematics - Integral Calculus
(4, 1, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'Indefinite Integrals & Antiderivatives', 'Integration is the reverse process of differentiation, allowing computation of accumulated quantities.', 'Power rule for integrals: ∫ x^n dx = (x^(n+1))/(n+1) + C (n ≠ -1).\n∫ (1/x) dx = ln|x| + C.\nConstant of integration C represents family of functions.', 'Quick Example', 'Evaluate ∫ (4x³ + 2x) dx', 'Result is x⁴ + x² + C.', 'Integration reconstructs position from velocity.', 'Always remember to add the integration constant C for indefinite integrals.'),
(4, 1, 2, 'Part 2: Definite Integrals', 'Fundamental Theorem of Calculus', 'The definite integral calculates the signed area under a curve between two limits.', 'Theorem: ∫[a to b] f(x) dx = F(b) - F(a) where F\'(x) = f(x).\nRepresents net area under curve between x=a and x=b.\nIntegration by parts: ∫ u dv = uv - ∫ v du.', 'Quick Example', 'Evaluate ∫[0 to 2] 2x dx', '[x²] from 0 to 2 = 2² - 0² = 4.', 'Area under y = 2x from 0 to 2 is exactly 4.', 'Use Riemann sums to conceptualize definite integration.'),

-- Chapter 5: Mathematics - Probability & Statistics
(5, 1, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'Sample Spaces & Probability Rules', 'Probability quantifies uncertainty by assigning numbers between 0 and 1 to outcomes of random experiments.', '0 ≤ P(A) ≤ 1 for any event A.\nComplement: P(A\') = 1 - P(A).\nAddition rule: P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A ∩ B).', 'Quick Example', 'Probability of rolling an even number on a 6-sided die', 'Outcomes: {2, 4, 6} out of 6. P = 3/6 = 0.5.', 'Equally likely outcomes lead to classical probability ratios.', 'Draw Venn diagrams to verify union and intersection operations.'),
(5, 1, 2, 'Part 2: Conditional Probability', 'Bayes Theorem & Distributions', 'Conditional probability measures the likelihood of an event given that another event has already occurred.', 'P(A|B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B).\nBayes Theorem: P(A|B) = [P(B|A) * P(A)] / P(B).\nNormal distribution forms a symmetric bell curve with mean μ and variance σ².', 'Quick Example', 'Calculate P(A|B) if P(A∩B)=0.2 and P(B)=0.5', 'P(A|B) = 0.2 / 0.5 = 0.4.', 'Bayes theorem allows updating beliefs in response to new evidence.', 'Bayesian inference powers spam filters and medical diagnostic algorithms.'),

-- Chapter 6: Computer Science - Introduction to Algorithms
(6, 2, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'Asymptotic Analysis & Big O', 'Algorithms are step-by-step procedures for solving computational problems. Big O notation characterizes their scalability.', 'Big O describes upper bound on growth rate.\nCommon complexities: O(1) < O(log n) < O(n) < O(n log n) < O(n²).\nWorst-case analysis guarantees performance bounds.', 'Quick Example', 'Find time complexity of single loop over array of size n', 'Runs n times -> O(n) linear time.', 'O(1) means constant time independent of input size.', 'Always identify the dominant term when simplifying Big O expressions.'),
(6, 2, 2, 'Part 2: Paradigms', 'Divide and Conquer Paradigm', 'Divide and Conquer breaks a problem into smaller subproblems, solves them recursively, and combines the results.', 'Step 1: Divide into independent subproblems.\nStep 2: Conquer recursively until base case.\nStep 3: Combine subproblem solutions.\nMaster Theorem solves recurrences of form T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n).', 'Quick Example', 'How does Merge Sort divide an array?', 'Splits array in half until single elements remain, then merges.', 'Merge sort guarantees O(n log n) time in all cases.', 'Ensure base cases terminate all recursive branches.'),

-- Chapter 7: Computer Science - Data Structures & Arrays
(7, 2, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'Static vs Dynamic Arrays', 'Arrays store contiguous elements of the same type in memory, enabling constant-time random access.', 'Random access by index: O(1) time.\nInsertion/deletion at end: O(1) amortized.\nInsertion at beginning/middle: O(n) due to shifting.', 'Quick Example', 'Why is arr[i] O(1)?', 'Memory address = Base + i * ElementSize.', 'Contiguous memory provides cache locality advantages.', 'Choose dynamic arrays when collection size varies dynamically.'),
(7, 2, 2, 'Part 2: Node-Based Structures', 'Linked Lists & Pointer Navigation', 'Linked lists store elements in scattered memory nodes connected by directional pointers.', 'Singly linked list: each node holds data and next pointer.\nDoubly linked list: holds previous and next pointers.\nO(1) insertion at head; O(n) access by index.', 'Quick Example', 'Insert node at head of linked list', 'new_node.next = head; head = new_node; (O(1) time).', 'No contiguous allocation needed; dynamic memory expansion.', 'Practice pointer manipulation without losing reference to head.'),

-- Chapter 8: Computer Science - Sorting & Searching Algorithms
(8, 2, 1, 'Part 1: Searching', 'Binary Search & Two Pointers', 'Searching locates target items within collections. Binary Search achieves logarithmic efficiency on sorted sequences.', 'Requires sorted collection.\nHalves the search space at every comparison: O(log n) time.\nTwo pointer technique optimizes search in sorted arrays.', 'Quick Example', 'Binary search in 1024 elements', 'At most log2(1024) = 10 comparisons needed.', 'Middle element comparison rules out 50% of candidates each step.', 'Beware of integer overflow: use mid = low + (high - low) / 2.'),
(8, 2, 2, 'Part 2: Sorting', 'Merge Sort & Quick Sort', 'Efficient comparison sorts achieve optimal O(n log n) average running time.', 'Merge Sort: Stable, O(n log n) guaranteed, requires O(n) auxiliary memory.\nQuick Sort: In-place, average O(n log n), pivot partitioning.\nWorst case for Quick Sort is O(n²) if pivot choice is poor.', 'Quick Example', 'Why choose Quick Sort over Merge Sort?', 'In-place sorting avoids O(n) extra RAM allocation.', 'Quick sort cache efficiency makes it faster in practice.', 'Randomized pivot selection avoids O(n²) worst-case behavior.'),

-- Chapter 9: Computer Science - Graph Theory & Trees
(9, 2, 1, 'Part 1: Trees', 'Binary Search Trees & Traversals', 'A tree is a hierarchical data structure of nodes connected by edges without cycles.', 'BST property: Left child < Parent < Right child.\nIn-order traversal of BST yields sorted elements.\nPre-order, In-order, Post-order are depth-first traversals.', 'Quick Example', 'In-order traversal order', 'Visit Left subtree -> Current Node -> Right subtree.', 'Balanced BSTs (AVL, Red-Black) guarantee O(log n) operations.', 'Unbalanced BSTs degrade to linked lists with O(n) search time.'),
(9, 2, 2, 'Part 2: Graphs', 'Graph Traversals: BFS & DFS', 'Graphs model relationships between arbitrary entities using vertices and edges.', 'Breadth-First Search (BFS) uses a Queue and finds shortest path in unweighted graphs.\nDepth-First Search (DFS) uses a Stack or recursion to explore deep branches.\nRepresented using adjacency matrix or adjacency list.', 'Quick Example', 'Queue in BFS', 'Enqueues starting node, visits all neighbors before moving deeper.', 'BFS guarantees minimal edge path in unweighted graphs.', 'Maintain visited set to prevent infinite loops in cyclic graphs.'),

-- Chapter 10: Computer Science - Dynamic Programming
(10, 2, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'Overlapping Subproblems & Memoization', 'Dynamic Programming optimizes recursive algorithms by caching answers to overlapping subproblems.', 'Applicable when problem has Optimal Substructure and Overlapping Subproblems.\nTop-Down approach: Recursion + Memoization (caching).\nTransforms exponential O(2^n) algorithms into polynomial O(n).', 'Quick Example', 'Fibonacci with memoization', 'Stores fib(k) in hash map on first compute, returns cached result thereafter.', 'Top-down keeps intuitive recursive structure while avoiding redundant work.', 'Look for repeat recursive calls with identical arguments.'),
(10, 2, 2, 'Part 2: Tabulation', 'Bottom-Up Tabulation & Knapsack Problem', 'Bottom-up DP fills a table iteratively from base cases up to the final target solution.', 'Bottom-Up avoids call stack overhead.\n0/1 Knapsack: Decide whether to include item i to maximize value under weight limit W.\nDP State: dp[i][w] = max(dp[i-1][w], value[i] + dp[i-1][w - weight[i]]).', 'Quick Example', 'Space optimization in DP', 'If state depends only on previous row, space reduces from O(n*W) to O(W).', 'Iterative tables enable optimal memory usage.', 'Define the state clearly before writing transition equations.'),

-- Chapter 11: Data Science - Exploratory Data Analysis
(11, 3, 1, 'Part 1: Data Cleansing', 'Data Cleansing & Missing Values', 'EDA investigates datasets to discover patterns, detect anomalies, and test hypotheses before modeling.', 'Identify missing values (MCAR, MAR, MNAR).\nImputation strategies: mean, median, mode, or KNN imputation.\nRemove duplicates and standardize categorical text.', 'Quick Example', 'When to use median over mean for imputation?', 'When data is skewed or contains extreme outliers.', 'Clean data is the foundation of trustworthy predictive models.', 'Always profile missingness percentages before dropping columns.'),
(11, 3, 2, 'Part 2: Summary Stats', 'Summary Statistics & Distribution Analysis', 'Descriptive statistics summarize central tendency, dispersion, and shape of dataset distributions.', 'Central tendency: Mean, Median, Mode.\nDispersion: Standard Deviation, Variance, Interquartile Range (IQR).\nSkewness measures asymmetry; Kurtosis measures tail heaviness.', 'Quick Example', 'Calculate IQR', 'IQR = Q3 (75th percentile) - Q1 (25th percentile).', 'Box plots visualize median, quartiles, and outlier thresholds (1.5 * IQR).', 'Pair descriptive statistics with visual histograms.'),

-- Chapter 12: Data Science - Data Visualization
(12, 3, 1, 'Part 1: Principles', 'Visual Storytelling & Chart Selection', 'Effective data visualization translates numerical patterns into intuitive visual forms.', 'Scatter plots: Show correlation between two numerical variables.\nBar charts: Compare categorical metrics.\nLine charts: Display trends over continuous time intervals.', 'Quick Example', 'Best chart for salary distribution', 'Histogram or KDE plot to show skewness and range.', 'Choose charts based on the relationship you want to communicate.', 'Avoid pie charts with more than 5 slices; use horizontal bar charts instead.'),
(12, 3, 2, 'Part 2: Advanced Visuals', 'Heatmaps, Correlation & Dashboards', 'Multivariate visual tools uncover hidden interactions across dozens of feature columns simultaneously.', 'Correlation heatmap displays Pearson coefficients from -1 to +1.\nBoxplots compare distributions across multiple categories.\nInteractive tools enable filtering and drill-down exploration.', 'Quick Example', 'Interpret correlation of -0.85', 'Strong negative linear relationship: as X increases, Y decreases.', 'Color intensity in heatmaps guides rapid pattern recognition.', 'Normalize axes to prevent misleading visual exaggerations.'),

-- Chapter 13: Data Science - Statistical Inference
(13, 3, 1, 'Part 1: Hypothesis Testing', 'Null Hypothesis & P-Values', 'Statistical inference draws conclusions about populations using representative sample observations.', 'Null Hypothesis (H0): No effect or no difference.\nAlternative Hypothesis (H1): An effect or difference exists.\nP-value: Probability of observing sample data if H0 is true.\nReject H0 when p-value < significance level α (typically 0.05).', 'Quick Example', 'If p-value = 0.02 and α = 0.05', 'p < α -> Reject H0; statistical significance detected.', 'Statistical significance does not always imply practical business significance.', 'Guard against p-hacking by pre-registering hypotheses.'),
(13, 3, 2, 'Part 2: Confidence Intervals', 'Confidence Intervals & Z-Tests', 'Confidence intervals provide a plausible range of values for unknown population parameters.', '95% Confidence Interval: Sample Mean ± 1.96 * (Standard Error).\nStandard Error = σ / √n.\nCentral Limit Theorem: Sample means approach normal distribution as n grows.', 'Quick Example', 'Impact of larger sample size on CI', 'Larger n decreases standard error and narrows the confidence interval.', 'Confidence intervals convey both effect size and precision.', 'Use t-distribution instead of z-distribution when population variance is unknown.'),

-- Chapter 14: Data Science - Linear & Logistic Regression
(14, 3, 1, 'Part 1: Linear Regression', 'Ordinary Least Squares & R²', 'Linear regression models linear relationships between dependent variable Y and independent variables X.', 'Equation: Y = β0 + β1*X + ε.\nMinimizes sum of squared residuals (OLS).\nR² score measures percentage of variance explained by the model.', 'Quick Example', 'Interpret R² = 0.82', '82% of variance in target variable is explained by features.', 'Assumptions: Linearity, independence, homoscedasticity, normality of residuals.', 'Check residual plots to verify linear modeling assumptions.'),
(14, 3, 2, 'Part 2: Logistic Regression', 'Sigmoid Function & Classification', 'Logistic regression predicts probability of categorical binary outcomes using the logistic sigmoid function.', 'Sigmoid function: σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(-z)).\nMaps any real number to probability between 0 and 1.\nDecision threshold (default 0.5) converts probability into class label.', 'Quick Example', 'If σ(z) = 0.78 with threshold 0.5', 'Predicted class is 1 (Positive).', 'Uses log-loss (binary cross-entropy) cost function.', 'Adjust threshold according to class imbalance or cost of false positives.'),

-- Chapter 15: Data Science - Feature Engineering
(15, 3, 1, 'Part 1: Preprocessing', 'One-Hot Encoding & Feature Scaling', 'Feature engineering transforms raw data into formats optimal for machine learning algorithms.', 'Categorical encoding: One-Hot for nominal, Label Encoding for ordinal.\nStandardization: (x - μ) / σ (zero mean, unit variance).\nMin-Max Normalization: (x - min) / (max - min) (scales to [0, 1]).', 'Quick Example', 'Why scale features before KNN or SVM?', 'Distance calculations are dominated by features with large magnitudes.', 'Fit scalers only on training split to prevent data leakage.', 'One-hot encoding high-cardinality features causes sparse dimensionality explosions.'),
(15, 3, 2, 'Part 2: Dimensionality', 'Dimensionality Reduction & PCA', 'Dimensionality reduction compresses feature spaces while preserving maximum variance.', 'Curse of dimensionality causes sparsity in high-dimensional spaces.\nPrincipal Component Analysis (PCA) finds orthogonal axes of maximal variance.\nReduces training time and prevents overfitting.', 'Quick Example', 'First Principal Component (PC1)', 'Captures the single largest proportion of dataset variance.', 'PCA transforms correlated features into uncorrelated components.', 'Standardize all features before applying PCA.'),

-- Chapter 16: Artificial Intelligence - Introduction to AI
(16, 4, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'Intelligence, Agents & Search', 'Artificial Intelligence studies rational agents that perceive environments through sensors and take actions through actuators.', 'PEAS framework: Performance measure, Environment, Actuators, Sensors.\nEnvironment types: Fully vs Partially observable, Deterministic vs Stochastic.\nSearch algorithms navigate state spaces from initial state to goal.', 'Quick Example', 'Self-driving car actuators', 'Steering wheel, accelerator, brakes.', 'Rational agents maximize expected performance based on percept history.', 'Distinguish between reflex agents and goal-based planning agents.'),
(16, 4, 2, 'Part 2: Heuristic Search', 'Heuristic Search & A* Algorithm', 'Informed search utilizes domain knowledge (heuristics) to find optimal paths rapidly.', 'Evaluation function: f(n) = g(n) + h(n).\ng(n) is exact cost from start to node n.\nh(n) is estimated cost from node n to goal.\nA* is complete and optimal if h(n) is admissible (never overestimates).', 'Quick Example', 'Straight-line distance heuristic', 'Always ≤ actual road distance, ensuring admissibility.', 'A* explores promising nodes first, avoiding exhaustive blind search.', 'Admissibility is required for tree search; consistency is required for graph search.'),

-- Chapter 17: Artificial Intelligence - Neural Network Basics
(17, 4, 1, 'Part 1: Neurons', 'Perceptrons & Activation Functions', 'Artificial neurons compute weighted sums of inputs, add bias, and apply non-linear activation functions.', 'Linear combination: z = w1*x1 + w2*x2 + ... + b.\nActivation introduces non-linearity: a = σ(z).\nCommon activations: ReLU (max(0, z)), Sigmoid, Tanh.', 'Quick Example', 'Why is non-linearity required in deep networks?', 'Without non-linear activations, multi-layer networks collapse to a single linear equation.', 'ReLU resolves vanishing gradient problems in deep architectures.', 'Use Leaky ReLU if dead neurons become problematic during training.'),
(17, 4, 2, 'Part 2: Architectures', 'Multi-Layer Perceptrons & Feedforward', 'MLPs stack input, hidden, and output layers to approximate complex mathematical functions.', 'Feedforward: Signal propagates forward through layer matrix multiplications.\nUniversal Approximation Theorem: 1 hidden layer with non-linear activation can approximate any continuous function.\nSoftmax activation in output layer produces probability distributions across multiple classes.', 'Quick Example', 'Softmax output for 3 classes', '[0.7, 0.2, 0.1] -> sum equals exactly 1.0.', 'Feedforward networks lack memory of previous sequential inputs.', 'Dropout layers prevent co-adaptation of hidden features.'),

-- Chapter 18: Artificial Intelligence - Gradient Descent
(18, 4, 1, 'Part 1: Optimization', 'Cost Functions & Loss Surfaces', 'Optimization minimizes loss functions by adjusting parameters in the direction of steepest descent.', 'Loss function measures discrepancy between prediction and ground truth.\nMSE Loss = (1/n) * Σ (y - ŷ)².\nGradient ∇J(w) points in direction of steepest increase.', 'Quick Example', 'Gradient Descent update rule', 'w = w - η * ∇J(w) where η is learning rate.', 'Negative gradient points toward the local minimum.', 'Select loss functions aligned with the learning task (MSE for regression, Cross-Entropy for classification).'),
(18, 4, 2, 'Part 2: Variants', 'Stochastic & Mini-Batch Gradient Descent', 'Variants of gradient descent balance computational speed with convergence stability.', 'Batch GD: Uses entire dataset; stable but slow for large datasets.\nStochastic GD (SGD): Updates per single sample; noisy but escapes shallow minima.\nMini-Batch GD: Updates per small batch (32-256 samples); optimal hardware parallelism.', 'Quick Example', 'Mini-batch size 64', 'Computes gradient over 64 samples at a time.', 'Mini-batch balances vectorization speed and gradient stability.', 'Momentum and Adam optimizers accelerate convergence over plain SGD.'),

-- Chapter 19: Artificial Intelligence - Backpropagation
(19, 4, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'Computational Graphs & Chain Rule', 'Backpropagation efficiently calculates partial derivatives of the loss with respect to all network weights.', 'Reverse-mode automatic differentiation over computational graphs.\nApplies chain rule backward from loss to input layers.\nReuses intermediate gradient computations efficiently.', 'Quick Example', 'Chain rule: ∂L/∂w', '∂L/∂w = (∂L/∂y) * (∂y/∂z) * (∂z/∂w).', 'Gradient calculations share sub-expressions across all layers.', 'Store forward pass activations in cache for reuse during backpropagation.'),
(19, 4, 2, 'Part 2: Challenges', 'Weight Updates & Vanishing Gradients', 'Deep networks face numerical instability during backpropagation due to repeated gradient multiplications.', 'Vanishing gradients: Gradients shrink to zero as they propagate backward through many sigmoid/tanh layers.\nExploding gradients: Gradients grow exponentially, destabilizing training.\nSolutions: ReLU activations, Batch Normalization, Residual Connections (ResNets).', 'Quick Example', 'Why does sigmoid cause vanishing gradients?', 'Sigmoid derivative maxes out at 0.25, shrinking gradients 4x per layer.', 'Proper weight initialization (He, Xavier) prevents premature saturation.', 'Gradient clipping caps norm values to prevent exploding gradients.'),

-- Chapter 20: Artificial Intelligence - Model Evaluation & Metrics
(20, 4, 1, 'Part 1: Classification Metrics', 'Confusion Matrix, Precision & Recall', 'Accuracy alone is misleading on imbalanced datasets; specialized metrics evaluate true performance.', 'Confusion Matrix: TP, FP, TN, FN.\nPrecision = TP / (TP + FP) (Quality of positive predictions).\nRecall = TP / (TP + FN) (Completeness of positive predictions).\nF1-Score = 2 * (Precision * Recall) / (Precision + Recall).', 'Quick Example', 'Cancer detection metric priority', 'High Recall is critical: minimize False Negatives.', 'Precision is vital when false alarms carry heavy costs.', 'Examine confusion matrices directly rather than relying on a single scalar metric.'),
(20, 4, 2, 'Part 2: Validation', 'ROC-AUC Curves & Cross-Validation', 'Robust validation strategies prevent overfitting and test generalization capabilities.', 'ROC curve plots True Positive Rate vs False Positive Rate across all thresholds.\nAUC (Area Under Curve) ranges from 0.5 (random guess) to 1.0 (perfect classifier).\nK-Fold Cross-Validation splits data into K subsets for repeated validation.', 'Quick Example', '5-Fold Cross-Validation', 'Trains on 4 folds, tests on 1 fold, repeated 5 times.', 'AUC is invariant to classification threshold and class prior distribution.', 'Stratified K-Fold preserves class balance percentages in every fold.'),

-- Chapter 21: Physics - Mechanical Waves
(21, 5, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'Wave Types & Characteristics', 'A wave is a disturbance that transfers energy through a medium without transferring physical matter.', 'Transverse waves: Particle oscillation is perpendicular to wave propagation (e.g., light, guitar string).\nLongitudinal waves: Particle oscillation is parallel to propagation (e.g., sound waves, compression springs).\nWavelength (λ) is distance between successive peaks.', 'Quick Example', 'Sound waves in air', 'Longitudinal compression and rarefaction waves.', 'Energy is transported; medium particles oscillate around equilibrium.', 'Electromagnetic waves can travel through a vacuum, whereas mechanical waves require a physical medium.'),
(21, 5, 2, 'Part 2: Wave Mechanics', 'Wave Speed, Frequency & Superposition', 'Wave velocity depends on the properties of the medium and the fundamental wave equation.', 'Fundamental wave equation: v = f * λ.\nPeriod T = 1 / f.\nPrinciple of Superposition: Net displacement is the algebraic sum of individual wave displacements.\nConstructive interference amplifies amplitude; destructive interference cancels amplitude.', 'Quick Example', 'Calculate wavelength of sound at f = 340 Hz in air (v = 340 m/s)', 'λ = v / f = 340 / 340 = 1.0 meter.', 'Wave speed is determined solely by medium properties (tension, density).', 'Standing waves form when two identical waves travel in opposite directions.'),

-- Chapter 22: Physics - Optics & Wave Motion
(22, 5, 1, 'Part 1: Geometric Optics', 'Reflection, Refraction & Snell\'s Law', 'Light behaves both as rays in geometric optics and as electromagnetic waves in wave optics.', 'Law of Reflection: Angle of incidence = Angle of reflection (θi = θr).\nSnell\'s Law of Refraction: n1 * sin(θ1) = n2 * sin(θ2).\nIndex of refraction n = c / v (speed of light in vacuum / speed in medium).', 'Quick Example', 'Light entering water (n=1.33) from air (n=1.0)', 'Bends toward the normal because light slows down in denser medium.', 'Total internal reflection occurs when angle of incidence exceeds critical angle θc.', 'Fiber optic cables utilize total internal reflection to transmit data across oceans.'),
(22, 5, 2, 'Part 2: Wave Optics', 'Interference & Diffraction', 'Wave nature of light is demonstrated through interference fringes and diffraction patterns around obstacles.', 'Young\'s Double-Slit Experiment proved light behaves as a wave.\nConstructive condition: d * sin(θ) = m * λ (m = 0, 1, 2...).\nDiffraction is the bending of waves around edges of obstacles.', 'Quick Example', 'Why do radio waves bend around buildings better than light?', 'Radio wavelengths are meters long (comparable to building sizes), causing significant diffraction.', 'Coherent light sources (lasers) have constant phase difference.', 'Diffraction gratings separate polychromatic light into spectral wavelengths with high precision.'),

-- Chapter 23: Physics - Thermodynamics & Heat
(23, 5, 1, 'Part 1: Principles', 'Heat Capacity & Thermodynamic Laws', 'Thermodynamics governs heat, work, temperature, and energy transformations.', 'First Law (Conservation of Energy): ΔU = Q - W.\nSpecific heat formula: Q = m * c * ΔT.\nZeroth Law defines thermal equilibrium and temperature concept.', 'Quick Example', 'Calculate heat to warm 2 kg water by 10°C (c = 4184 J/kg°C)', 'Q = 2 * 4184 * 10 = 83,680 Joules.', 'Internal energy depends solely on temperature for ideal gases.', 'Distinguish carefully between heat (energy in transit) and temperature (average kinetic energy).'),
(23, 5, 2, 'Part 2: Second Law', 'Carnot Engine & Entropy', 'The Second Law states that total entropy of an isolated system never decreases over time.', 'Entropy (S) is a measure of molecular disorder and energy dispersal.\nCarnot efficiency limit: η_max = 1 - (T_cold / T_hot) in Kelvin.\nNo real heat engine can achieve 100% thermal efficiency.', 'Quick Example', 'Carnot engine between 600K and 300K', 'Efficiency = 1 - 300/600 = 0.50 (50% maximum efficiency).', 'Heat naturally flows spontaneously from hotter to cooler bodies.', 'Absolute zero (0 Kelvin) cannot be reached in a finite number of thermodynamic steps.'),

-- Chapter 24: Physics - Electromagnetism
(24, 5, 1, 'Part 1: Electrostatics', 'Coulomb\'s Law & Electric Fields', 'Electric charges produce force fields that interact through Coulomb\'s inverse-square law.', 'Coulomb\'s Law: F = k * (|q1 * q2|) / r² (k = 8.99 * 10^9 N m²/C²).\nElectric Field E = F / q (force per unit positive test charge).\nElectric potential V = k * q / r (measured in Volts).', 'Quick Example', 'Force between like charges vs opposite charges', 'Like charges repel; opposite charges attract.', 'Electric field lines originate on positive charges and terminate on negative charges.', 'Gauss\'s Law relates electric flux through closed surfaces to enclosed charge.'),
(24, 5, 2, 'Part 2: Electrodynamics', 'Magnetic Induction & Faraday\'s Law', 'Moving electric charges generate magnetic fields, and changing magnetic flux induces electromotive force.', 'Lorentz Force on charge: F = q(E + v × B).\nFaraday\'s Law of Induction: EMF = -dΦB / dt.\nLenz\'s Law: Induced current opposes the change in magnetic flux that produced it.', 'Quick Example', 'Electric generators', 'Rotating coils inside magnetic fields produce alternating electrical current.', 'Transformers rely on changing AC magnetic flux to step voltage up or down.', 'Maxwell\'s equations synthesize electricity, magnetism, and light into one unified theory.'),

-- Chapter 25: Physics - Quantum Mechanics Basics
(25, 5, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'Wave-Particle Duality & Photons', 'At microscopic scales, light and matter exhibit both wave-like and particle-like properties simultaneously.', 'Photoelectric Effect proved light consists of discrete energy packets (photons).\nPhoton energy: E = h * f (h = 6.626 * 10^-34 J s).\nde Broglie wavelength of matter: λ = h / p.', 'Quick Example', 'Calculate photon energy for f = 5 * 10^14 Hz', 'E = (6.626 * 10^-34) * (5 * 10^14) = 3.31 * 10^-19 Joules.', 'Electrons produce diffraction patterns identical to light waves.', 'Classical physics fails to explain blackbody radiation and atomic emission spectra.'),
(25, 5, 2, 'Part 2: Quantum States', 'Heisenberg Uncertainty & Wavefunctions', 'Quantum mechanics replaces deterministic trajectories with probability density wavefunctions.', 'Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle: Δx * Δp ≥ ℏ / 2.\nCannot simultaneously know exact position and exact momentum.\nWavefunction ψ(x): Probability density of finding particle is |ψ(x)|².\nSchrödinger Equation governs time evolution of quantum wavefunctions.', 'Quick Example', 'Quantum tunneling', 'Particles have non-zero probability of passing through barriers higher than their energy.', 'Quantum states exist in superposition until measurement collapses the wavefunction.', 'Quantum tunneling enables modern flash memory and nuclear fusion in stars.'),

-- Chapter 26: Chemistry - Atomic Structure
(26, 6, 1, 'Part 1: Foundations', 'Subatomic Particles & Isotopes', 'Atoms are the fundamental building blocks of chemical matter, composed of protons, neutrons, and electrons.', 'Protons (+1 charge, in nucleus), Neutrons (0 charge, in nucleus), Electrons (-1 charge, in orbitals).\nAtomic number Z = number of protons (determines chemical element).\nMass number A = protons + neutrons.\nIsotopes have identical atomic number Z but different mass numbers A.', 'Quick Example', 'Carbon-12 vs Carbon-14', 'Both have 6 protons; Carbon-12 has 6 neutrons, Carbon-14 has 8 neutrons.', 'Chemical behavior is governed by valence electron configurations.', 'Radioactive isotopes decay exponentially according to their characteristic half-lives.'),
(26, 6, 2, 'Part 2: Quantum Orbitals', 'Bohr Model & Quantum Shells', 'Electrons inhabit quantized energy levels characterized by principal quantum numbers and orbital shapes.', 'Bohr model: Electrons occupy discrete circular energy levels (n = 1, 2, 3...).\nElectrons emit light when falling from higher to lower energy levels (ΔE = hf).\nOrbital types: s (spherical, 2 electrons), p (dumbbell, 6 electrons), d (10 electrons), f (14 electrons).', 'Quick Example', 'Electron configuration of Oxygen (Z=8)', '1s² 2s² 2p⁴.', 'Aufbau principle fills lowest energy subshells first.', 'Hund\'s rule fills degenerate orbitals singly with parallel spins before pairing.'),

-- Chapter 27: Chemistry - Periodic Table
(27, 6, 1, 'Part 1: Architecture', 'Groups, Periods & Periodic Trends', 'The periodic table arranges elements by increasing atomic number, reflecting recurring periodic chemical trends.', 'Vertical columns are Groups (elements share valence electrons and chemical reactivity).\nHorizontal rows are Periods (correspond to occupied principal quantum shells).\nMetals on the left, non-metals on the right, metalloids along the diagonal divide.', 'Quick Example', 'Alkali metals (Group 1)', 'Lithium, Sodium, Potassium: 1 valence electron, highly reactive with water.', 'Noble gases (Group 18) possess stable full octets and are chemically inert.', 'Periodic arrangement allows predicting chemical properties of unobserved elements.'),
(27, 6, 2, 'Part 2: Trends', 'Electronegativity & Ionization Energy', 'Periodic trends explain atomic dimensions, electron attraction, and bond-forming propensities.', 'Atomic radius decreases across a period, increases down a group.\nIonization energy is the energy required to remove an electron from gaseous atom.\nElectronegativity is the ability of an atom in a molecule to attract shared electrons.\nFluorine is the most electronegative element (Pauling scale 4.0).', 'Quick Example', 'Which has higher electronegativity: Fluorine or Chlorine?', 'Fluorine has higher electronegativity due to smaller atomic radius.', 'Ionization energy peaks at noble gases.', 'Effective nuclear charge increases left to right across periods, pulling orbitals tighter.'),

-- Chapter 29: Chemistry - Acids and Bases
(29, 6, 1, 'Part 1: Theories', 'Arrhenius & Brønsted-Lowry Theory', 'Acids and bases are fundamental chemical classifications defined by hydrogen ion transfers.', 'Arrhenius: Acids produce H+ ions in water; bases produce OH- ions.\nBrønsted-Lowry: Acids are proton (H+) donors; bases are proton (H+) acceptors.\nConjugate acid-base pairs differ by a single proton.', 'Quick Example', 'HCl + H2O -> H3O+ + Cl-', 'HCl is acid, Cl- is conjugate base; H2O is base, H3O+ is conjugate acid.', 'Amphoteric substances (like water) can act as both acid and base.', 'Strong acids (HCl, HNO3, H2SO4) dissociate completely in aqueous solutions.'),
(29, 6, 2, 'Part 2: Quantitative Acid-Base', 'pH Scale & Buffer Solutions', 'The pH scale quantifies hydrogen ion concentration logarithmically from 0 to 14.', 'pH = -log10[H+].\npH < 7 is acidic; pH = 7 is neutral; pH > 7 is basic (alkaline).\npH + pOH = 14 at 25°C.\nBuffer solutions resist changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added.', 'Quick Example', 'Calculate pH of solution with [H+] = 10^-3 M', 'pH = -log10(10^-3) = 3 (Acidic).', 'Each 1-unit change on the pH scale represents a tenfold change in acidity.', 'Blood buffer system (carbonic acid/bicarbonate) tightly maintains human blood at pH 7.35-7.45.'),

-- Chapter 30: Chemistry - Chemical Reactions
(30, 6, 1, 'Part 1: Stoichiometry', 'Balancing Equations & Conservation of Mass', 'Chemical reactions transform reactant substances into products while conserving total atomic mass.', 'Law of Conservation of Mass: Total mass of reactants equals total mass of products.\nReaction types: Synthesis, Decomposition, Single Displacement, Double Displacement, Combustion.\nStoichiometric coefficients balance element atom counts on both sides of reaction.', 'Quick Example', 'Balance: H2 + O2 -> H2O', '2 H2 + O2 -> 2 H2O.', 'Subscripts cannot be changed; only coefficients can be adjusted.', 'Mole ratio from balanced equation governs reactant consumption.'),
(30, 6, 2, 'Part 2: Kinetics & Equilibrium', 'Reaction Kinetics & Le Chatelier\'s Principle', 'Reaction rates depend on collision frequency, activation energy, and chemical equilibrium states.', 'Collision Theory: Molecules must collide with sufficient energy (Ea) and correct orientation.\nCatalysts lower activation energy without being consumed in the reaction.\nLe Chatelier\'s Principle: Systems at equilibrium shift to counteract external perturbations.', 'Quick Example', 'Effect of increasing pressure on N2 + 3 H2 ⇌ 2 NH3', 'Shifts right toward fewer gas moles (2 vs 4 moles), increasing ammonia yield.', 'Temperature increases always favor endothermic reaction direction.', 'Dynamic equilibrium means forward and reverse reaction rates are exactly equal.')

